From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 02:34:23 +0300
Subject: nouveau: no fence reference work when the fence doesn't change

nv30_state_validate re-references the context's current fence on every buffer bound to a draw
(vertex buffers, the fragment program) once per draw, and nouveau_transfer_write and
nouveau_copy_buffer do the same per buffer write. Between two kicks the fence is the same, so
nearly all of these calls find *ref already pointing at it. nouveau_fence_ref still took the
screen's fence lock and did an atomic increment and decrement that cancel out: four atomic
read-modify-writes, two of them with a full barrier. On the PS3's PPE (in-order, lwarx/stwcx.
and sync are slow) nouveau_fence_ref and _nouveau_fence_ref took 2-11% of the CPU in games,
most in immediate-mode draws (X-Moto, ClassiCube).

Both functions now return at once when *ref already holds the fence. The reference counts and
*ref end the same as before; only the cancelling increment and decrement and the lock are
skipped.

Forwarded: no

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nouveau_fence.c b/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nouveau_fence.c
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nouveau_fence.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nouveau_fence.c
@@ -341,6 +341,10 @@ nouveau_fence_work(struct nouveau_fence *fence,
 void
 _nouveau_fence_ref(struct nouveau_fence *fence, struct nouveau_fence **ref)
 {
+   /* Already referenced: the increment and decrement below would cancel out. */
+   if (*ref == fence)
+      return;
+
    if (fence)
       p_atomic_inc(&fence->ref);
 
@@ -359,6 +363,12 @@ nouveau_fence_ref(struct nouveau_fence *fence, struct nouveau_fence **ref,
 {
    struct nouveau_fence_list *fence_list = &screen->fence;
 
+   /* Nothing changes when *ref already holds this fence, the common case for a buffer used by
+    * every draw of a batch: skip the lock and the atomics.
+    */
+   if (*ref == fence)
+      return;
+
    simple_mtx_lock(&fence_list->lock);
    _nouveau_fence_ref(fence, ref);
    simple_mtx_unlock(&fence_list->lock);
